#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fba09f7-6fb0-5d6c-8424-e721e8481780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468166} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfcaae9-18c3-556e-876b-dbe83dd13637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fba09f7-6fb0-5d6c-8424-e721e8481780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468166} :answer-field/id #uuid "1111e0c2-88ed-5b55-afd9-e459c3ebe34f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468804} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3eda578-a166-50a0-a055-3491f285c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468168} {:answer/id #uuid "162f9645-e89a-5701-ad26-d9ed38cd8047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "162f9645-e89a-5701-ad26-d9ed38cd8047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468169} :answer-field/id #uuid "c285d321-3810-5e14-84d2-43bf04bf70f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b017c3c-7510-5e3d-b988-1b2e3d35ce99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468170} {:answer/id #uuid "178b845d-847d-59e6-9f16-2bed3653076e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b017c3c-7510-5e3d-b988-1b2e3d35ce99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6cf479-04ac-5307-9b8b-3196833e508d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a5c031b-68ff-5d30-80f0-79dec42f9067" :question/math-academy-id "q-75729" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03abb9a7-2b48-52fb-a796-934e69dbf835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468172} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4cfef1-5371-5f3e-857e-64eebc7ac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac4cfef1-5371-5f3e-857e-64eebc7ac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468173} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6c909f-63c0-5135-bbf9-4d267137c73a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6aec76-6d7b-5744-8ce8-2ebf6affd726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468174} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ff5181-6f4d-55aa-a9ac-4242973df9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ff5181-6f4d-55aa-a9ac-4242973df9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468175} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47d1666-97cf-5ba1-8e08-40775e23e294" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1035b0df-44b2-5fe0-afef-c28f18fd27c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468176} {:answer/id #uuid "61af2a66-29c7-569c-b2ed-0c194422b56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61af2a66-29c7-569c-b2ed-0c194422b56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468177} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4acdf0c-ddba-5b17-9a40-e4b3170b2b16" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca01d454-a476-5d37-9896-aa972462aa23" :question/math-academy-id "q-76208" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abfd15c8ed134a30ad719f6a857268427511e7f1f15ecfe0d27e5026b5401a83.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3)$\n- $x\\in [3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,3).$ It is continuous on $(-2,3)$ and also right continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[3,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc598710-e322-5db2-83bc-e862528237bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468178} {:answer/id #uuid "2575c1e4-9a08-598b-a4a6-ba47900d821f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2575c1e4-9a08-598b-a4a6-ba47900d821f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468179} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0bcebac-d48f-5104-a76e-f3bf41ace939" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468810} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc2d913-15d6-5e57-8ef1-ee1745d3a95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468180} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf0d8fc-630b-5