db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f920bbd-9273-58b0-886f-3ee7ba08f9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469120} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d770fd8-7ca8-5dfc-970f-69a91d10d56f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f36feb8-418f-5686-b06d-68d66e729361" :question/math-academy-id "q-79830" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$\n- **III.** $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y(x)={x}^{4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759