3e946bcb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81816" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,0),(1,5),(2,-2).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n- **I.** $(-2,2)$\n- **II.** $(0,3)$\n- **III.** $(-1,-5)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,0),(-1,-5),(-2,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdcbfd3a-e8a0-5cc7-b990-7a59096f3316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469260} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b17aa7-7706-56e2-9568-216559170626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 1