-5410-965e-98b9294f009d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e244cae-49ac-5727-b109-5857ab506f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-89887" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- **II.** $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- **III.** $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7336966f-8336-50bb-9351-9f946f8a93c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469554} {:answer/id #uuid "2f19c276-4dd0-5818-a7e8-e0a8c790f637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f19c276-4dd0-5818-a7e8-e0a8c790f637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469555} :answer-field/id #uuid "52922013-663f-5486-a5b8-b73490734552" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470389} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67d3ff23-6cd5-5077-95e9-4bf353e699f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469556} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fbe06b-4f03-5ee5-8347-c57fabde826e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67d3ff23-6cd5-5077-95e9-4bf353e699f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469556} :answer-field/id #uuid "c58572a0-d4f3-5a9c-ae1f-3e90b3667207" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4704dc4d-5c8e-52aa-9ff6-c866e334a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469558} {:answer/id #uuid "afd9e88a-8078-53b1-a2cf-af582cabf5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4704dc4d-5c8e-52aa-9ff6-c866e334a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469558} :answer-field/id #uuid "662a2990-175d-596c-894a-52061ccb6fc3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0272b49a-4d96-50b3-ac67-e22a2911e738" :question/math-academy-id "q-89888" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- **II.** $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- **III.** $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
